<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Int J Emerg Med</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>International Journal of Emergency Medicine</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">1865-1372</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1865-1380</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Springer-Verlag</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19384651</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2657257</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">67</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s12245-008-0067-6</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Clinical Images</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Primary pyomyositis caused by ca-MRSA</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Lo</surname><given-names>Bruce M.</given-names></name><address><phone>+1-757-3883397</phone><fax>+1-757-3882885</fax><email>brucelo1@yahoo.com</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Fickenscher</surname><given-names>Benjamin A.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><aff id="Aff1">Department of Emergency Medicine, Eastern Virginia Medical School, Raleigh Building, 600 Gesham Drive, Room 304, Norfolk, VA 23507 USA </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>17</day><month>10</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>17</day><month>10</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>12</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><volume>1</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>331</fpage><lpage>332</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>16</day><month>4</month><year>2008</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>10</day><month>9</month><year>2008</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; Springer-Verlag London Ltd 2008</copyright-statement></permissions><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; Springer-Verlag London Ltd 2008</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><p>A 22-year-old male with sickle-cell trait presented with a 1-week history of progressive pain and swelling in his left posterior thigh. He denied any trauma, fever, travel outside of the United States, or intravenous drug usage. On examination, he was afebrile at 36.8 &#x000b0;C (98.3 &#x000b0;F) with stable vital signs. His examination was significant for exquisite tenderness, erythema, induration, and swelling of the posterior thigh (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). A computed tomography scan of the extremity was obtained that showed a 5.5&#x02009;cm &#x000d7; 4.4&#x02009;cm &#x000d7; 20-cm fluid collection within the biceps femoris muscle (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>). The patient underwent open drainage of the abscess and was placed on linezolid. Wound and blood cultures were consistent with community-associated methicillin-resistant <italic>Staphylococcus aureus</italic> (ca-MRSA).<fig id="Fig1"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;1</label><caption><p>Erythema and swelling of posterior thigh (<italic>arrows</italic>)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12245_2008_67_Fig1_HTML" id="MO1"/></fig><fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;2</label><caption><p>CT of leg showing a large fluid collection (<italic>arrows</italic>)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12245_2008_67_Fig2_HTML" id="MO2"/></fig></p><p>Pyomyositis is an acute bacterial infection of the muscles characterized by subacute abscess formation. While pyomyositis is rare in temperate climates, the incidence has been increasing, with the majority having comorbidities causing an immunocompromised state, such as HIV and diabetes [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>]. Sickle-cell disease has also been implicated as a risk factor for developing pyomyositis [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR4">4</xref>].</p><p><italic>Staphylococcus aureus</italic> has been implicated in the majority of cases [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>], and recently, ca-MRSA has been found to be an important causative agent in pyomyositis [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">6</xref>]. Because of the rarity of pyomyositis in temperate climates, the diagnosis can be difficult to make, and the patient&#x02019;s symptoms and exam can easily be mistaken for other diagnoses, such as cellulites [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">7</xref>]. Physicians should consider pyomyositis in patients with severe muscle tenderness in areas of apparent cellulitis and recognize ca-MRSA as an emerging cause of this potentially devastating disease.</p></body><back><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>References</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Crum</surname><given-names>NF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Bacterial pyomyositis in the United States</article-title><source>Am J Med</source><year>2004</year><volume>117</volume><fpage>420</fpage><lpage>28</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.amjmed.2004.03.031</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15380499</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="CR2"><label>2.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Millar</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Page</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Paterson</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>MRSA pyomyositis complicating sickle cell anaemia</article-title><source>Clin Lab Haematol</source><year>2001</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>329</fpage><lpage>32</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1046/j.1365-2257.2001.00415.x</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11703417</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="CR3"><label>3.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Akman</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Ostrov</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Varma</surname><given-names>BK</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Pyomyositis: report of three patients and review of the literature</article-title><source>Clin Pediatr</source><year>1996</year><volume>35</volume><fpage>397</fpage><lpage>401</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1177/000992289603500803</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="CR4"><label>4.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Konnur</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Boris</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Nield</surname><given-names>LS</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Non-tropical pyomyositis in pediatric and adult patients</article-title><source>W V Med J</source><year>2007</year><volume>103</volume><fpage>22</fpage><lpage>3</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18159840</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="CR5"><label>5.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ruiz</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name><name><surname>Yohannes</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Wladyka</surname><given-names>CG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pyomyositis caused by methicillin-resistant <italic>Staphylococcus aureus</italic></article-title><source>N Engl J Med</source><year>2005</year><volume>352</volume><fpage>1488</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1056/NEJM200504073521417</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15814887</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="CR6"><label>6.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pannaraj</surname><given-names>PS</given-names></name><name><surname>Hulten</surname><given-names>KG</given-names></name><name><surname>Gonzalez</surname><given-names>BE</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Infective pyomyositis and myositis in children in the era of community-acquired, methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection</article-title><source>Clin Infect Dis</source><year>2006</year><volume>43</volume><fpage>953</fpage><lpage>60</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1086/507637</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16983604</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="CR7"><label>7.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fox</surname><given-names>LP</given-names></name><name><surname>Geyer</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Grossman</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pyomyositis</article-title><source>J Am Acad Dermatol</source><year>2004</year><volume>51</volume><fpage>308</fpage><lpage>314</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.jaad.2004.01.060</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15280855</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>